# R0 / R1 response circuit proposal and source boundary

These ignored modules implement mathematical public equations of the frozen
IdealFrontierSim proposal. They do not sample a witness, recover masks, read a
hidden seed, condition on witness validity or assume a new high-mask stream.
Challenges 1/2, four consistency responses, six coefficients and d are retained
prescribed input coins. They are not ordinary hash queries. W0 separately
programs those values in the existing chronological overlay. The existing
single immutable base F contract remains unchanged; R0 and R1 make zero reads.

`r0_machine.VoleResponse(ell,mul,mul_u64)` consumes bit registers in this order:
Delta256, Q[ell] (256 bits each), qbar[10], challenge1[2112], Utilde[4]. It
copies Vtilde[4] into an arbitrary separate 1024-bit target and cleans all work.
The input contains no d because this equation does not use d; the enclosing
machine retains d and all prescribed fields for R1 and W0.

The exact source mask order matters. `proof.rs::vole_hash` processes Q in row
order, then qbar[0..6], and passes qbar[6..10] as its four final additive
terms. Each folded coordinate runs four full-field Horner products and one
64-bit-point product. Final output j is
`r_j*y_0 + y_(j+1) + qbar_(6+j) + Delta*Utilde_j`. All eight full-field
finalization products and every XOR are present. qbar[0..6] are also the six
QuickSilver mask coefficients; there is no reordering at the V1 interface.

`r1_machine.QuickSilverResponse(role,list_count,mul,mul_u64)` consumes Delta,
Q[ell], qbar[10], challenge2[832], six transmitted coefficients, d[ell], and
relation-public bits. The latter match the P0/P1 owner's output order:

- Registration: commitment block200B, authorization block200B, commitment128B,
  authorization64B.
- Vote: commitment/nullifier/authorization blocks200B each, list128*S bytes,
  nullifier128B, authorization64B. S is a fixed accepted public shape from3
  to1000, matching `VotePublic.has_shape()`. Generic selector helper controls
  may use synthetic smaller shapes; the actual role factory rejects them.

Its output is A0[256], followed by a two-bit error code and a64-bit source
count/constraint index. Codes are0=success,1=count,2=bad index,3=degree overflow;
A0 is zero on the explicitly totalized error branch. The source returns a
typed error instead of an A0 on that branch. The caller must inspect the tag.
Internal source degree/count errors depend on the fixed program structure,
not field values, and their exact precedence and first-error latch are kept.
Public request validation and invalid VotePublic shape rejection belong to
P0; a supported R1 factory requires its canonical static shape.

The compiler computes Delta^0..7, explicitly retains Q_i+d_i*Delta once per
coordinate, and runs every Booleanity constraint followed by the actual
source relation stream. Registration has35,840 witness coordinates and36,736
relation constraints, totaling72,576. Vote has53,504 and55,427, totaling108,931.
The relation includes all320 column parities,320 theta terms,1,600 theta
applications, source rho/pi mapping and required nonlinear output fields of
each round. Eleven two-round groups are compared to committed state keys;
the twelfth group computes the constrained output prefix. This is field-key
QuickSilver evaluation with degree alignment, not ordinary Boolean Keccak.

Vote selectors include both32-element prefix/sum streams, all S selector
products, the range constraint and all1,024*S public-bit-controlled product
copies for the commitment multiplexer. No public-list scan is a free RAM
lookup. The fixed scan uses a narrowly scoped normalization: source selecting
no public bits returns degree0 zero; the circuit's sum is degree2 zero. Both
give identical zero after the immediate addition to a degree4 output, even
when Delta=0. Nonempty branches retain degree2. The helper requires that exact
degree4 consumer; no other observer receives the normalized intermediate tag.

Each admitted constraint is lifted to degree7 and fed to both ZKHash Horner
lanes in source order. Final A0 includes both challenge-weighted accumulators,
all six qbar_j*Delta^j for j=0..5, and all six transmitted coefficient products
with Delta^j for j=1..6. Zero challenges, zero Delta and arbitrary field inputs
are accepted; no nonzero division or retry is used.

The shared `FieldBuilder` emits XOR, bit embedding, conditional field copy,
constants and registered multiplication kernels. Every kernel invocation
copies its two operands to separate clean input registers, invokes the pinned
clean field circuit and reverses the copies. Thus equal/aliased source operands
do not silently violate the kernel's disjoint interface. Operand copies cost
1,024 CNOTs for full multiplication and640 for a64-bit multiplier, in addition
to the previously reviewed field-kernel cost. A single1,023-bit kernel scratch
region is shared; all256-bit SSA results remain until the outer output copy
and reverse traversal. Even constants/zero field registers are included in
retained history. There is no opaque unit-cost field operation.

The count-only builder traverses the same program without retaining operation
objects. `emit_clean` records and emits the actual schedule; it does not replace
the circuit by the count. Full actual-size elementary streams have not been
executed. The exploratory `response-actual-shapes.json` retains exact draft
hashes and exploratory full structural counts for both role lengths and
synthetic vote sizes1/1024. Those two vote counts predate the actual-factory
shape correction and are not accepted source requests. The separate current
`response-accepted-shapes.json` retains final counts for accepted sizes3/1000;
it is not final capture or a native measurement.

Tests independently evaluate polynomial products, five-lane VOLEHash order,
Delta products, key correction, degree lifts, both ZKHash lanes, six mask and
coefficient terms, error precedence and zero-selector normalization. A field
round is compared against an independent forward rho/pi coordinate model.
Clean gate assembly is interpreted with deliberately simple full-width test
kernels, including arbitrary output targets, aliased source multiplication,
untouched inputs, every work bit and inverse restoration. Real field arithmetic
is checked separately using the pinned concrete adapter; toy kernels establish
no field semantics. Deliberately omitting uncompute leaves dirty work.

Selected Rust source correspondence is bound by the parent machine's existing
coverage inventory (`proof`, `universal_hash`, `quicksilver` and its verifier,
role relations, layout, selector, keccak_relation and field sources). The
generic kernel registry remains an explicit trusted contract; integration must
use the authenticated `FieldKernel` adapters and watch these executing modules.
Source hashes are not a Rust compiler equivalence proof or a Python dependency
closure. Whole-request wiring, input provenance/preparation, chronology and
retained joint state remain outside these components. All full coverage,
resource-vector, primitive-advantage, independent-review and qualification
claims remain false pending their separate requirements.

